WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.350
human fat, whose goal is to escape the pit
and take the place of their counterpart.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:06.689
Picture this – if you’ll excuse that foreshadowing
pun: you’re at your local city mall in Ohio,

00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:09.300
a place that’s never still.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.070
Every time you go there, no matter what time
of day or day of the week, it’s always full

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:19.410
of people, all bustling about, shopping, eating,
dashing past one another.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:24.400
Maybe, presumably like everyone else, you
came in to get some specific item.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.940
Or you might be just browsing.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.789
Heck, you could be there for a casual date,
or to grab a bite to eat at the food court.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:34.420
That’s when you remember, you need to have
your picture taken.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:39.920
It could be your passport is about to expire,
or your driver’s license hasn’t been updated

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.440
since you moved.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.089
Whatever the reason, lucky for you there’s
a photo booth right here in the mall.

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:49.909
‘Take Your Own Photo’ boasts the sign
on the outside of the cramped cubicle, a list

00:00:49.909 --> 00:00:54.460
of instructions below describing just how
quick and easy the process is.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.530
You take a look at the price list, feed the
right amount of money into the slot, and take

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.170
a seat inside.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.780
Pulling the curtain closed, you straighten
your back, looking directly into the lens.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.310
A short countdown appears on the screen; three…
two… one.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:13.710
A flash bursts from the bulb above the lens,
filling the booth, and leaving those white

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.640
spots floating in your vision for a few moments.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.560
You head back out of the cubicle, waiting
to pick up the glossy paper with your photos

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.259
on it as it starts rapidly printing them off.

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:27.100
You wanted a two-by-two, four total pictures
of your face.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.329
But then, as it finishes printing and you
inspect the pictures, turning the paper in

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:34.159
your hands, you’re met with a surprise…

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:37.180
an unnerving one to say the least.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.850
The pictures don’t show your face, in fact,
it barely looks like a human face at all.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.560
It has your clothes, and your hair, but the
whole image has been warped.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.759
Not just out of focus, but completely disproportional.

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:53.540
Annoyed that you spent your money only to
receive a highly distorted image, you wander

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.820
off to find another booth, throwing the unusable
picture in the nearest trash can.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:04.020
Now, sure, you might write off something like
that as just an odd occurrence, a freak of

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.740
technology caused by faulty machinery in desperate
need of repair.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.950
But what you don’t know – in fact, you
won’t even realize until it’s too late

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.170
– is what really just happened.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.500
When you sat down and that flash went off,
taking your picture and printing it off as

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.150
a warped, unrecognizable mess, something was
born.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.900
Located at the end of a series of long, winding
concrete stairs and kept out of sight behind

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.530
doors marked ‘Keep Out’ and ‘Staff Only’,
is a lower level of the mall.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:37.790
Hidden in this dark, off-limits area, that
no member of the public is permitted to access,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:43.450
there is a service door, somewhere in the
southwest corner of the third subbasement.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.660
Just taking a passing look at it, you could
easily imagine nothing all that interesting

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.010
behind it – like a power generator or a
storage room.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.810
But if you were to push it open and venture
inside, you’d stumble upon something you

00:02:54.810 --> 00:03:01.020
never would have expected in your wildest
dreams… or, more accurately, your most disturbing

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.020
nightmares.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.670
Beyond that service door is a cavern, hardly
the first thing you’d assume to be secreted

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.060
away underneath a city shopping mall.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.850
Although, if you’re into cave jumping, it
would be ill-advised to take a spelunking

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.340
adventure down there.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:20.000
The entire cavern isn’t actually part of
our world, it’s an extradimensional space.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.830
Think of it as its own little pocket universe
that has somehow become connected to ours

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.210
and can now be accessed through this doorway.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:32.910
If it helps, you can even picture our universe
as a big soap bubble, with a slightly smaller

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.420
bubble attached to it.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:39.360
In actuality, it’s infinitely more complicated
and looks nothing like two bubbles of different

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.210
sizes, but like we said, if it helps to picture
it that way then go right ahead.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:49.030
If you were brave enough – or naïve enough
– to venture deeper into this cave and not

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:53.600
be put off by its walls that look like they’ve
been carved from pure limestone, then you

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.470
would have to watch your step to avoid a nasty
fall.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:03.210
Within the large cave space is a deep, dark
pit, a rocky conduit in the ground, seemingly

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.300
leading down into the Earth.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.040
But, taking a closer look, the walls of this
pit aren’t made of rock, they don’t even

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.140
match the limestone that the rest of the cavernous
room looks to be cut from.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:19.740
The surface of the pit is coated in a slimy
substance, closer to something biological,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:23.050
coming from a living thing rather than being
carved out of rock.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.790
Depending on your familiarity with the texture,
you might even say this substance is not all

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:32.140
that far off from the appearance and feel
of human fat tissue.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.910
Although you probably shouldn’t reach in
to try to touch it for yourself, least of

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:40.370
all because the walls of the pit are constantly
secreting a corrosive substance like acid,

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:44.970
that could easily break down your body by
its molecules, painfully burning you until

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.390
you melted down into soup.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:52.680
But there’s something else down there; lots
of somethings, as a matter of fact.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.620
And the moment you took that photo, sitting
in the safety of the booth in the mall above,

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.410
a new something found itself born into the
pit.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:05.920
It looks almost exactly like you, but its
face is all wrong.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.440
You could even say distorted, like a photograph
that didn’t quite come out right.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:16.260
The abnormalities in its face could take the
form of lacerations; cuts and open wounds,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:20.900
or bloody welts, like looking in a mirror
at a twisted, injured version of yourself.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.910
Or, this imperfect copy might have growths
bulging out from its head, covering its facial

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:29.780
features to make it virtually unrecognizable
as a clone of you.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:36.400
Then again, maybe it’ll just come out missing
everything: no eyes, mouth, nose, just a blank,

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.139
expressionless mask on a body otherwise identical
to yours.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:46.100
And what does this… thing, this clone do
now that it finds itself in a pit, filled

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:51.750
with other writhing, imperfect copies of everyone
that’s ever sat to have their picture taken

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:52.910
in that photo booth?

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.380
Well, what would you do?

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.820
You would try to get out, as quickly as possible.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.500
And that’s what it will start doing.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.460
While you’re going about the rest of your
day, maybe stopping off at a few more stores

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.700
in the mall, or complaining to the customer
service staff about that faulty photo booth,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.210
it’s already started climbing.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.630
Its fingernails are digging into that fleshy
tissue that coats the walls of the pit, as

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:20.010
the copy drags its imperfect body upwards,
crawling over the others around it that are

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.000
trying to do the same.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.670
There’s no telling just how many of them
are down there, how many misshapen heads and

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:31.950
deformed faces your clone has had to barge
past and climb over as they all pile deep

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.350
down within their hole in the ground.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.160
And what’s the worst part of all this?

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.200
It’s not just the climb, the fact that your
copy has to fight its way out of that pit,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.250
making the near-impossible ascent to make
it to the surface.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:49.020
No, the worst part is that you have no idea
it’s happening.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.199
You’re completely oblivious that a distorted
clone of you is raking its nails against walls

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:57.970
of fat tissue, clambering up from underneath
the ground.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:05.200
And even more so, you have no idea what it
wants to do when it gets free and finds you…

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.830
The SCP Foundation had long since been keeping
an eye on the anomaly, that they themselves

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:13.240
had designated as SCP-715.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:17.200
Given that the photo booth itself was connected
to the extradimensional space buried deep

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.290
below the mall, removing it isn’t really
an option.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:24.280
Sure, they could easily have cordoned it off,
and put up signs saying the booth was out

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.400
of order to deter members of the public from
trying to use it.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:33.060
But this was the SCP Foundation, after all,
they needed to run tests, figure out exactly

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:36.070
what this anomaly did and how it ticked.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:40.510
Gerald Patton had been a rising star at the
Foundation, a promising researcher who had

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:43.940
long been proving his mettle since he first
became part of the organisation.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:48.650
For a time, the higher-ups at the Foundation
had planned to attach the researcher to the

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:54.550
SCP-2090 project, studying a basketball player
who somehow gained anomalous powers.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:59.410
However, hearing about the photo booth in
the Ohio city mall, he turned down this request

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.949
to instead join the team researching SCP-715.

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:08.640
Certain high-level staff of the SCP Foundation
noted this to be strange, Patton had never

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.090
turned down a promotion before, as the SCP-2090
project would have offered him better pay

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:16.120
and more vacation time.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:20.420
In order to observe the photo booth closely
without alerting the public to their presence,

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:24.870
Foundation staff on site were disguised to
blend in with civilians and some posed as

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.280
retail staff employed at the mall.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.780
Come closing time, once everyone else that
worked there or who had been shopping throughout

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:36.579
the day had finally left, the Foundation researchers
were free to run their tests and investigate

00:08:36.579 --> 00:08:38.180
the anomalous photo booth.

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:43.050
It was the night of a routine sweep, all staff
members on site were required to test the

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:45.980
surrounding area for reality-warping anomalies.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:52.660
The sweep was mandatory for the entire team
assigned to SCP-715, yet quite unusually,

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.369
Researcher Patton didn’t report in for the
procedure.

00:08:55.369 --> 00:08:59.600
The higher-ups at the Foundation decided to
let the indiscretion slide though, after all,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.499
there were a lot of personnel involved in
the process, so Patton was let off the hook.

00:09:04.499 --> 00:09:08.879
In fact, the few disparities in Patton’s
behaviour only became more apparent when Doctor

00:09:08.879 --> 00:09:13.709
Agatha Rights began to more closely collate
and process the files and information on the

00:09:13.709 --> 00:09:15.540
personnel on-site.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.999
She was the Research Head on the SCP-715 project
and quickly noticed a few things didn’t

00:09:20.999 --> 00:09:23.959
add up about Researcher Gerald Patton.

00:09:23.959 --> 00:09:29.589
His rejection of the offer to join the SCP-2090
project and refusal to attend a routine reality-bending

00:09:29.589 --> 00:09:33.749
anomaly sweep might have seemed like unrelated
incidents at first.

00:09:33.749 --> 00:09:38.220
But both would have brought the researcher
into contact with technology that the Foundation

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.160
used specifically to detect any distortions
to reality.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.749
If he had taken the promotion, or been present
for the sweep, he too would have been within

00:09:46.749 --> 00:09:49.399
the scope of this specialized detection equipment.

00:09:49.399 --> 00:09:54.449
Yet, both times, he managed to avoid being
put in such a position.

00:09:54.449 --> 00:09:59.920
What the hell was Patton hiding… and what
would show up if a detector was pointed at

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:00.920
him?

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:05.079
Doctor Rights was growing ever more suspicious
of Researcher Patton and his behavior.

00:10:05.079 --> 00:10:10.600
However, it was when she learned he had secretly
tested the photo booth himself that she decided

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:15.170
to take matters into her own hands in order
to get to the bottom of what Gerald was up

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:16.170
to.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.110
She had to mull it over, and think about things
cautiously.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:22.529
She couldn’t confront him directly, at least
not yet.

00:10:22.529 --> 00:10:27.420
Even the evidence she already had – that
Patton had seemingly twice avoided situations

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:32.149
involving the use of reality distortion detection
equipment – wasn’t enough to prove for

00:10:32.149 --> 00:10:34.569
certain he was hiding something.

00:10:34.569 --> 00:10:39.379
Both instances could have been caused by something
completely unrelated, Gerald could have rejected

00:10:39.379 --> 00:10:46.220
his promotion purely because he was more interested
in SCP-715 than joining the SCP-2090 project.

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:50.519
And even Foundation personnel needed sick
days, or skipped work because of a hangover

00:10:50.519 --> 00:10:54.769
without telling anyone, which could explain
why he was absent during the sweep of the

00:10:54.769 --> 00:10:55.769
mall.

00:10:55.769 --> 00:11:01.110
But Agatha wasn’t convinced, it felt like
the two instances lined up too closely, they

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:05.600
felt far too similar to be an utterly random
coincidence.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.839
Even if Gerald wasn’t up to anything dangerous,
the only way to know for sure was to observe

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:12.329
him, but without drawing attention to it.

00:11:12.329 --> 00:11:17.579
He’d deny anything, act completely differently,
maybe even flat out lie if he knew someone

00:11:17.579 --> 00:11:19.360
was listening or watching.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:24.329
Agatha was forced to think in a way that was
as calculated as she was certain Gerald had

00:11:24.329 --> 00:11:25.329
been.

00:11:25.329 --> 00:11:30.009
His quarters were located in a secluded part
of the subbasement, a level above where the

00:11:30.009 --> 00:11:32.069
fleshy pit could be found.

00:11:32.069 --> 00:11:37.119
Almost all the Foundation personnel assigned
to SCP-715 slept somewhere on-site, their

00:11:37.119 --> 00:11:40.779
presence there masked from other ordinary
mall employees.

00:11:40.779 --> 00:11:45.730
Taking care not to be detected, they had installed
doors with a key card system, meaning only

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:49.589
members of their own staff could access the
living quarters under the mall.

00:11:49.589 --> 00:11:53.569
If any civilians happened to stumble upon
the Foundation and discover them, then all

00:11:53.569 --> 00:11:58.629
it took was the careful application of amnestics
to ensure that they wouldn’t remember a

00:11:58.629 --> 00:11:59.629
thing.

00:11:59.629 --> 00:12:02.630
Beeping her key card at the door, Agatha slipped
in to the living area.

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:06.800
It mostly consisted of a narrow corridor,
with a row of doors either side.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:12.019
Behind each one was a cramped room, each only
big enough for one person, a bunk to sleep

00:12:12.019 --> 00:12:14.399
in and a sink with a mirror above it.

00:12:14.399 --> 00:12:19.220
Gerald’s room was the furthest from the
entrance, right down at the end of the corridor,

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:21.559
as far from everyone else as he could get.

00:12:21.559 --> 00:12:25.759
Although, it also meant if he came back from
testing early, then he’d be more likely

00:12:25.759 --> 00:12:29.610
to catch Agatha leaving his room and walking
back along the row.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:33.920
It would be hard to come up with an excuse
as to what she was doing anywhere near his

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:39.959
quarters, especially as her room was behind
the very first door, right next to the entrance.

00:12:39.959 --> 00:12:43.950
All this was rushing through Doctor Rights’
mind as she squeezed into Patton’s room;

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:48.459
in actual fact, the interior space of them
was so limited they were even smaller than

00:12:48.459 --> 00:12:49.629
most ship cabins.

00:12:49.629 --> 00:12:54.619
Her hand had been clasped around the handle
of a metal case which she set down on Gerald’s

00:12:54.619 --> 00:12:55.619
empty bed.

00:12:55.619 --> 00:12:59.769
Unclasping the clips that held it closed,
Agatha opened up the case and took out what

00:12:59.769 --> 00:13:02.749
was held inside the inner layer of protective
foam.

00:13:02.749 --> 00:13:08.100
It was a Fulmann-Breaker Anomalous Optical
Enhancement device, a piece of the specialized

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:12.970
apparatus that the Foundation’s researchers
used to record and identify any person or

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:16.439
object that might be exhibiting anomalous
properties.

00:13:16.439 --> 00:13:20.749
It utilized state-of-the-art optical enhancement
technology and could be used to detect whether

00:13:20.749 --> 00:13:25.810
a subject was causing any distortion to the
reality around it, by accessing visual spectrums

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:31.269
that were normally imperceivable to the naked
human eye; or sometimes even unobservable

00:13:31.269 --> 00:13:32.769
in this dimension.

00:13:32.769 --> 00:13:37.850
Agatha looked around the tiny cabin; given
the limited space, she was also short on options

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:41.600
as to where she could hide the device without
it being easily noticed.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:46.809
It was imperative that Researcher Patton had
no idea he was under observation.

00:13:46.809 --> 00:13:50.829
Doctor Rights looked over to the sink in the
corner: the mirror just above it.

00:13:50.829 --> 00:13:55.509
Sure, it was a tactic taken straight out of
the big book of spy novel clichés, but it

00:13:55.509 --> 00:13:56.509
would work.

00:13:56.509 --> 00:14:00.019
The advanced spectrum capabilities of the
Optical Enhancement device would be able to

00:14:00.019 --> 00:14:02.199
record Patton through the glass.

00:14:02.199 --> 00:14:06.389
Luckily, the entire living quarters had been
pretty hastily built, which meant it took

00:14:06.389 --> 00:14:09.889
little effort for Agatha to loosen the screws
holding the mirror in place.

00:14:09.889 --> 00:14:14.220
But that’s when she heard the distinctive
beep of the door to the living quarters, followed

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:17.239
by gradual footsteps getting closer and closer.

00:14:17.239 --> 00:14:22.089
It might not have been Gerald, but there was
an equal chance that it could have been.

00:14:22.089 --> 00:14:25.519
Acting as fast as she could, Agatha placed
the Optical Enhancement device on the wall

00:14:25.519 --> 00:14:29.509
and covered it with the mirror, frantically
trying to screw it back into place as quick

00:14:29.509 --> 00:14:30.509
as she could.

00:14:30.509 --> 00:14:34.389
The echoing noise of someone approaching sounded
as if they were almost right outside the cabin

00:14:34.389 --> 00:14:39.610
door by the time Doctor Rights had tightened
the screws enough to keep the mirror in place.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:44.209
Giving a quick final scan of the room, making
sure nothing looked too conspicuous, she grabbed

00:14:44.209 --> 00:14:48.920
the empty case and smoothed out the duvet
where it had been laying, leaving no trace

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:50.839
that she had ever been there.

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:54.850
Her heart already racing, it almost exploded
right out of her chest as she slipped back

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.079
out of the door to Patton’s cabin…

00:14:57.079 --> 00:15:02.679
only to see him walking down the narrow corridor
directly towards his room, towards her.the

00:15:02.679 --> 00:15:04.189
nearest trash can.

00:15:04.189 --> 00:15:08.170
“Doctor Rights,” Gerald greeted with a
staged smile, though the suspicion on his

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:12.379
face was so unmistakable that that it could
probably show up clearly on camera.

00:15:12.379 --> 00:15:14.470
“Something I can help you with?”

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:18.540
“Patton,” Agatha gave a polite nod back
to the researcher; lifting the case behind

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:19.540
her back.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:23.569
The one upside of the tight corridor was that
Patton was so close to her he couldn’t see

00:15:23.569 --> 00:15:28.459
what she was hiding; the obvious downside
being he might be close enough to clock Doctor

00:15:28.459 --> 00:15:30.509
Rights’ suspicion of him.

00:15:30.509 --> 00:15:32.299
“Did you need something?”

00:15:32.299 --> 00:15:33.299
Patton chuckled.

00:15:33.299 --> 00:15:34.499
“Sorry, it’s just…

00:15:34.499 --> 00:15:36.420
what were you doing in my quarters?”

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:40.550
Her heart was still racing, desperately trying
to form a plausible excuse somewhere in her

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:41.550
mind.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:45.149
Agatha’s free hand reached into her pocket,
something brushing against her fingertips;

00:15:45.149 --> 00:15:46.629
it would have to do.

00:15:46.629 --> 00:15:48.249
“You dropped this!”

00:15:48.249 --> 00:15:51.930
she exclaimed, pulling out a twenty-dollar
bill and handing it to Gerald, a small price

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:53.339
to pay to keep her subterfuge.

00:15:53.339 --> 00:15:57.959
“Earlier on, in the mall, near SCP-715,
I saw it fall out of your pocket.

00:15:57.959 --> 00:16:02.829
I had other matters to attend to down at…
anyway, I wanted to make sure you got it back.”

00:16:02.829 --> 00:16:06.980
“Oh, thank you,” Gerald blinked in surprise,
taking the money.

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:10.939
As she squeezed past him and walked out of
the living quarters, Agatha could barely stop

00:16:10.939 --> 00:16:12.269
her hands from shaking.

00:16:12.269 --> 00:16:16.939
Although, those tremors of nerves would become
the trembling of absolute horror when she

00:16:16.939 --> 00:16:20.790
saw the footage that the Optical Enhancement
device collected.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.420
Patton was one of the creatures from the pit.

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:27.679
It didn’t seem possible; he didn’t even
have clearance to know about the pit, and

00:16:27.679 --> 00:16:32.749
any of the clones – or SCP-715-As – that
managed to claw their way up to the surface

00:16:32.749 --> 00:16:38.019
were immediately terminated by on-site security
– the instances becoming known as SCP-715-Bs

00:16:38.019 --> 00:16:40.359
if they made it that far.

00:16:40.359 --> 00:16:45.139
Yet, somehow Patton, or whatever he really
was, had made it through undetected.

00:16:45.139 --> 00:16:49.149
But that was far from the worst discovery
Doctor Rights would make.

00:16:49.149 --> 00:16:55.329
She used a number of the same Anomalous Optical
Enhancement devices to observe the SCP-715-A

00:16:55.329 --> 00:16:59.879
creatures within the pit only to find that
she and the other researchers had made a grave

00:16:59.879 --> 00:17:00.989
mistake.

00:17:00.989 --> 00:17:05.130
The SCP-715-As… they were all humans.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:09.589
What the Foundation had initially thought
were the distorted, imperfect clone copies

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:15.559
of everyone who had used the SCP-715 photo
booth, were actually the originals.

00:17:15.559 --> 00:17:21.289
They had been trying to climb back up because
they wanted to come home, their places taken

00:17:21.289 --> 00:17:24.480
by SCP-715-Bs.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:29.540
But none of those people – now twisted,
disfigured, and unrecognizable – were able

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:31.880
to explain to the Foundation who they were.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:36.950
And they’d been shot, left to fall back
into the pit and die.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:42.120
Acting fast, Doctor Rights updated the classification
of SCP-715 to Keter, although this was later

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:43.990
rescinded back to ‘Safe’.

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:48.980
The creature that looked like Researcher Patton
was apprehended and brought in for interrogation.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:54.670
After learning the truth behind SCP-715, the
photo booth was secured and locked up permanently.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:18:00.740
But, as of now, the project investigating
SCP-715 has been put on hold.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:05.519
No-one at the Foundation wants to admit the
mistake, that they now have an extradimensional

00:18:05.519 --> 00:18:10.600
pit full of innocent, distorted people…
or that they have no idea where all their

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:12.950
body-swapped counterparts are…

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:18.960
Now go and check out “SCP-1733 SEASON OPENER”
for another tale of another anomaly caught

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:22.990
on film – this time, it’s a basketball
that you won’t be able to stop watching…

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:28.039
Or, if you’d rather further your investigation
into some more paranormal photography, then

00:18:28.039 --> 00:18:33.770
we’ve got just the video for you: “SCP-978
THE DESIRE CAMERA”!

